{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And your Hebrew servant, etc.</b> The verse's saying <b>and your servant</b> with the addition of the vav comes to alert you that a commandment preceded it — this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded from the word ״לְךָ״, that a court has no authority to sell a Hebrew slave except to an Israelite. And this is the measure of the verse <b>ki yimmacher</b> (when he is sold) — meaning, when he needs to be sold, it is to you alone that he may be sold. And it adds a second ruling: ״and your servant shall serve six years.״" "<b>And in the seventh</b>, etc. — and it did not say ״and he shall go out in the seventh,״ but rather it intended to say that sometimes he may serve even in the seventh year: for instance, if the slave fled — even though six years of his servitude were completed, he must complete the six years of servitude. And so Rambam ruled in chapter 2 of the Laws of Slaves, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded it (Kiddushin 16b) from what the verse says in Mishpatim: ״six years he shall serve.״ And perhaps we expound from ״six years he shall serve״ to teach that if he was ill for four years he must complete (Kiddushin 17a) and serve six; and from the precision of ״and in the seventh״ we learn that if he fled, he must complete the servitude even if he fled during a single year."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:33.952996176Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}